@@ -27,6 +27,8 @@ The platform is **multi-app**: RCA is one App produced from an in-code template
 - **AgentConfig** — one entry the FE picker offers. `{name, model, system_prompt, suggestions, allowed_tools, env, sandbox_image, idle_timeout_seconds}`. Pure data (msgspec.Struct). Not a specstar resource — lives in deploy config (`Settings.agent_configs` / `config.yaml`), not in the DB. Built by the catalog from a `Preset` + a usage entry.
 - **AgentConfigCatalog** — the runner-time directory of available `AgentConfig`s + the resolver that picks one for a given investigation. Owns: `list()` / `get(name)` / `default()` / `resolve(attached_name, template_profile)`. The resolve cascade is `attached → template → default` plus the template-appendix composition onto `system_prompt`. Lives in `agent/config_catalog.py`. The FE's `/agent-configs` route reads from one of these; `create_investigation` auto-attach reads `default()` from it; per-turn agent selection reads `resolve(...)` from it. **The catalog replaces** the `LitellmAgentRunner.list_configs / get_config` Protocol methods and the four `app.py` closures (`_runner_configs`, `_runner_get_config`, `_first_agent_config`, `_resolve_agent_config`) that previously did the same work in pieces.
 - **AgentToolContext** — the per-turn context the agent's tools receive (sandbox, filestore, ask_kb bridge, etc.). Two flavours (RCA / KB) share one dataclass; "wrong flavour" is signalled by optional fields being `None`. Carries the resolved `AgentConfig` per turn.
+- **Sub-agent definition** — a markdown file with YAML frontmatter (`name` / `description` / `tools`, body = the sub-agent's system prompt) at `.agent/<name>/AGENT.md`. Same dual source as a **Skill**: an App profile ships some (`apps/<slug>/profiles/<p>/.agent/`, read from the package, never seeded) and the item's own workspace may add or override them by name (read live, so a file-IDE edit lands on the next turn). `tools:` is a REQUEST — `apps/subagents.load_subagents` clamps it to what the turn is itself allowed to do, the same rule `save_workflow` applies to an agent-written workflow. A malformed / nameless / renamed / oversized definition is skipped, never fatal — which is why an agent authors one through **`save_subagent`** rather than `write_file`: that tool owns the format, the slug and the path, and CHECKS the round trip (it renders, parses the result back with the real loader, and refuses when they disagree — owning the format is not the same as owning what a caller puts into it, and a character blacklist would have missed the body-at-cap case), so a definition it saved is always one it can call. Tools outside the turn's own ceiling are refused BY NAME instead of quietly trimmed (a sub-agent that believes it holds `exec` fails in a way its caller cannot read).
+- **`run_agent`** — the tool the main agent delegates a whole sub-task with (`run_agent(agent_type, prompt)` → the sub-agent's report). Built when the turn has definitions **or** holds `save_subagent` — the #537 rule bars a tool that can only ever refuse, and an agent that can fill the list itself is not that case. Name resolution falls back to reading `.agent/` LIVE when the turn's frozen index misses, so a sub-agent saved mid-reply is callable in that same reply (what `read_skill` has always done); the two lists are unioned, never swapped, so an empty live read cannot erase the advertised names. The sub-turn runs through `api/subagent_run.run_agent_task`, which builds the child context by REPLACING fields on the parent's — workspace, identity and ceilings carry over, prompt / tools / history do not — so `tool_authz` keeps gating the sub-agent as the same speaker. Non-recursion is structural: the child's `run_agent` seam is `None` and its `subagent_defs` empty, so the tool is never built for its turn. Distinct from the KB **sub-agent bridge** below, which a sub-agent may still use.
 - **AgentRunner** — the Protocol that drives one turn. Post-catalog refactor, knows nothing about the picker — just runs a turn given a context. Implementations: `LitellmAgentRunner` (prod), `ScriptedAgentRunner` (tests).
 
